{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012Xf87kDd2pHzvXVi2hpvag","type":"tool_result","content":"# Hydration\n\nHydration means writing HTML as a string on the server, then\ncompiling JS for the page to add interactivity. It gets you faster initial page\nload, and the behavior of a\n[single page app](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/SPA).\n\n<details><summary><h2>Contents</h2></summary>\n\n<!-- toc -->\n\n- [Running this example](#running-this-example)\n- [Files in this folder](#files-in-this-folder)\n- [`.render`](#render)\n- [`.html`](#html)\n  * [Self-closing tags](#self-closing-tags)\n  * [`children`](#children)\n  * [Returning `html` from `.render`](#returning-html-from-render)\n- [Nesting components and lists](#nesting-components-and-lists)\n- [Stripping `.render` from the client bundle](#stripping-render-from-the-client-bundle)\n  * [1. Vite plugin](#1-vite-plugin)\n  * [2. esbuild plugin](#2-esbuild-plugin)\n  * [3. `microbuild` CLI](#3-microbuild-cli)\n  * [4. `transform` function](#4-transform-function)\n- [Keeping `.render` on the client](#keeping-render-on-the-client)\n\n<!-- tocstop -->\n\n</details>\n\n\n## Running this example\n\nAll scripts run from the repo root. Each one that uses the Vite config\nbuilds the Node entries (`npm run build:node`) first, because the config\nimports the stripping plugin from `dist/vite.js`.\n\n- `npm run start:hydration` -- start the Vite dev server with the Hono\n  app mounted under `/example-hydration/`, and open the index.\n- `npm run ssr:hydration` -- print one assembled page to stdout. Pass a\n  route after `--`, for example `npm run ssr:hydration -- /list`. The\n  default is the index.\n- `npm run build:hydration` -- production build: a client build and a\n  server build into `build/client` and `build/server` (gitignored).\n- `npm run serve:hydration` -- run the built production server. Set\n  `PORT` to choose the port.\n- `npm run test:hydration` -- build, then test the app with\n  `app.request()` and the built listener.\n- `npm run test:hydration-dev` -- start the dev server and fetch the\n  routes over HTTP.\n- `npm run typecheck:hydration` -- type-check `server/` with Node types.\n- `npm run test:e2e` -- Playwright tests against the production server.\n\nThe Playwright browser is a one-time install:\n\n```sh\nnpx playwright install chromium\n```\n\nThe routes are `/example-hydration/` (a counter with a nested badge),\n`/example-hydration/list` (badges rendered from an array), and\n`/example-hydration/notice` (a button that inserts a client-rendered\nnotice). Any other path under the prefix returns a 404 page.\n\n## Files in this folder\n\nThe folder is split by where each module runs.\n\n- `index.html` -- the checked-in page scaffold, and the Vite HTML entry.\n  `<main>` holds exactly one `<!--microtags:main-->` marker, which the\n  server replaces with the route body. Nothing writes to this file.\n- `vite.config.ts` -- this example's own Vite config: aliases, the\n  stripping plugin, the Hono dev server, and both production builds.\n\n`client/` runs only in the browser:\n\n- `index.ts` -- the client entry. It imports the components, which\n  registers them and hydrates the page, then runs a few runtime\n  assertions and prints the results on the page. The assertions check\n  that `Counter.render` and `Badge.render` are `undefined` in the\n  browser bundle and that `Notice.render` is still a function.\n- `reveal.ts` -- removes the class that hides the page until it has\n  hydrated.\n- `index.css` -- page-level styles.\n\n`components/` runs on both sides:\n\n- `counter.ts` -- a counter with increment and decrement buttons. Its\n  `.render` returns `html`, nests a `Badge` that shows the current\n  count, and passes `children` through.\n- `badge.ts` -- the smallest possible component.\n- `notice.ts` -- keeps its render function on the client with\n  `{ client: true }`.\n- `counter.css`, `badge.css` -- component styles. Under plain esbuild\n  they load as empty modules (`--loader:.css=empty`).\n\n`pages/` runs on both sides:\n\n- `layout.ts` -- the navigation and `<main>` around a page. Each link\n  binds `aria-current` to the `route` signal.\n- `index.ts`, `list.ts`, `notice.ts`, `not-found.ts` -- one page\n  function per route, each taking the app state.\n- `routes.ts` -- the route table. `pageFor(route)` returns the page for\n  a route, or the not-found page.\n\n`server/` runs only in Node:\n\n- `app.ts` -- the Hono app and its routes. It has no Node imports and\n  takes an injectable scaffold loader.\n- `scaffold.ts` -- loads the scaffold and replaces the marker.\n- `listen.ts` -- the production server. It loads the built scaffold,\n  mounts the app, and serves built assets.\n- `ssr.ts` -- the stdout render command.\n- `tsconfig.json` -- type-checks this folder with Node types.\n\n---\n\n## `.render`\n\nWrite your components with a\n[`.render`](../README.md#builderrenderfn-options) method.\nThe function passed to `.render` gets called with a `context` object.\n\nThis package exposes two paths, `microtags/esbuild` and `microtags/vite` that\nprovide plugins that will strip the `.render` function from the client build,\nwhich keeps the clientside bundle small.\n\nAdd a `.render` call in the method chain, before `.setup`. The render function\nreceives `props` (plain values, not signals), `refs`, `TAG`, `children`,\nand `attrs`, and should return the host element's inner HTML.\n\n`props` and `attrs` are both attributes on the tag, split by whether the\nname was declared in `.withProps`. A declared name, like `start` below, is\na prop: it is coerced to its declared type (`5`, a number) and is what\n`.setup` reads on the client. Any other attribute, like `class` or `id`,\nis passed through as a plain string in `attrs`. Both are written to the\nhost tag. `attrs` is there so the render function can react to them, for\nexample to forward `class` to an inner element.\n\n```ts\n// one file for markup and behavior\nimport { signal } from 'alien-signals'\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\n\nexport const HydraCounter = define('hydra-counter')\n    .withProps(p => ({\n        // `start` attribute is coerced to a number\n        start: p.number()\n    }))\n    .withRefs(r => ({\n        display: r.one<HTMLDivElement>(),\n        inc: r.one<HTMLButtonElement>(),\n    }))\n    .render(({ props, refs, attrs }) => {  // serverside only\n        // `class` is not a declared prop, so it arrives in `attrs`\n        const cls = attrs.class ?? ''\n        return `\n            <div data-ref=\"${refs.display}\" class=\"${cls}\">${props.start}</div>\n            <button data-ref=\"${refs.inc}\">+</button>`\n    })\n    .setup(ctx => {  // clientside only\n        const count = signal(ctx.props.start())\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            ctx.refs.display.textContent = String(count())\n        })\n        ctx.on(ctx.refs.inc, 'click', () => count(count() + 1))\n    })\n```\n\n## `.html`\n\nOn the server, components should be rendered with the\n[`html`](../README.md#microtagshtm)\n[tagged template literal](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Template_literals#tagged_templates),\nor you can call the static `HydraCounter.render(props)` directly.\n`html` knows how to expand every component,\neg `<${MyComponent} start=\"5\"><//>`, into its content, which is the HTML\nreturned by `.render`. The `<//>` is `htm` shorthand for the closing tag,\nso you do not have to repeat the component name.\n\n```ts\n// runs in Node\nimport { html } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { HydraCounter } from './counter.js'\n\nexport function page ():string {\n    return html`<main>\n        <${HydraCounter} start=${5} class=\"big\"><//>\n    </main>`\n}\n```\n\nThe output is the expanded HTML, with props serialized\nas attributes, so `.setup`, which runs on the client, can read the\n`start` attribute. The undeclared `class` attribute is also written to\nthe host tag, and the render function copied it onto the inner `div`.\n\n```html\n<main>\n    <hydra-counter start=\"5\" class=\"big\">\n        <div data-ref=\"display\" class=\"big\">5</div>\n        <button data-ref=\"inc\">+</button>\n    </hydra-counter>\n</main>\n```\n\nCalling the static method directly takes the same two parameters as\nseparate arguments:\n\n```ts\nHydraCounter.render({ start: 5 }, { attrs: { class: 'big' } })\n```\n\nSee [`builder.render`](../README.md#builderrenderfn-options) for the\nfull reference.\n\n### Self-closing tags\n\nInside `html` a component can be written as `<${HydraCounter} start=${5} />`,\nwhich is what the `pages/` modules and `client/index.ts` do. That is fine\nbecause `html` always emits a real closing tag in the output. It is only\nin hand-written HTML that a custom element must not be self-closed, since\nthe browser would parse everything after it as its children.\n\n### `children`\n\nAnything between the open and close tags is passed to the render function\nas `children`, already rendered to a string. `counter.ts` emits\n`${children}` at the end of its markup, so this:\n\n```ts\nhtml`<${Counter} start=${5}><p>Extra content</p><//>`\n```\n\nputs the paragraph after the buttons. The static form is\n`Counter.render({ start: 5 }, { children: '<p>Extra content</p>' })`.\n\n### Returning `html` from `.render`\n\nThe render function can return `html` instead of a plain string. Every\ninterpolated string is escaped, so a prop value containing `<` comes out\nas `&lt;`, which a plain template literal does not do. Nested components\ngo inline as `<${Badge} ... />`. `components/counter.ts` is written\nthis way.\n\nThe one thing to watch is `children`. It reaches the render function as\nan already-rendered string, so passing it through `html` would escape the\nmarkup. Wrap it in `raw()` to insert it as is.\n\n```ts\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\nimport { html, raw } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { Badge } from './badge.js'\n\nexport const Counter = define('hydra-counter')\n    .withProps(p => ({\n        start: p.number(),\n    }))\n    .withRefs(r => ({\n        display: r.one<HTMLDivElement>(),\n        inc: r.one<HTMLButtonElement>(),\n    }))\n    .render(({ props, refs, children }) => {\n        return html`\n            <div data-ref=${refs.display} class=\"count\">${props.start}</div>\n            <${Badge} label=${String(props.start)} />\n            <button data-ref=${refs.inc}>+</button>\n            ${raw(children)}`\n    })\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        // ...\n    })\n```\n\n`html` writes attribute values in quotes for you, so `data-ref=${refs.inc}`\nis enough. Rendering `<${Counter} start=${5}><p>Extra</p><//>` then gives:\n\n```html\n<hydra-counter start=\"5\">\n    <div data-ref=\"display\" class=\"count\">5</div>\n    <hydra-badge label=\"5\"><span>5</span></hydra-badge>\n    <button data-ref=\"inc\">+</button>\n    <p>Extra</p>\n</hydra-counter>\n```\n\nWithout `raw()` the last line would be `&lt;p&gt;Extra&lt;/p&gt;`.\n\n## Nesting components and lists\n\nA render function can nest other components. `counter.ts` puts a\n`Badge` inline in its `html` template. The badge also carries the\ncounter's `current` ref, so `.setup` can update the badge text when the\ncount changes:\n\n```ts\nimport { html } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { Badge } from './badge.js'\n\n// ...\n    .render(({ props, refs, children }) => {\n        return html`\n            <div role=\"status\" class=\"count\">...</div>\n            <div class=\"current\">\n                <span>Current:</span>\n                <${Badge}\n                    data-ref=\"${refs.current}\"\n                    class=\"number\"\n                    label=${String(props.start)}\n                />\n            </div>\n            ...`\n    })\n```\n\n`data-ref` and `class` are not declared props of `Badge`, so they are\nwritten to the `<hydra-badge>` host tag as plain attributes.\n\nBecause `Badge` and `microtags/htm` are only used inside `.render`, they\nare dead code on the client once the render function is stripped. The\n`cascade` option removes those imports too. Without it they stay in the\nbundle. See [Stripping](#stripping-render-from-the-client-bundle).\n\nFor a list, map over the array. Inside an `html` template the array is\nflattened for you:\n\n```ts\nhtml`<ul>\n    ${items.map(item => html`<li><${Badge} label=${item}/></li>`)}\n</ul>`\n```\n\nInside a plain template string, join it yourself:\n\n```ts\n`<ul>\n    ${items.map(item => html`<li><${Badge} label=${item} /></li>`).join('')}\n</ul>`\n```\n\n\n## Stripping `.render` from the client bundle\n\nWithout a build step the render function ships to every browser. The\ntransform function in [`microtags/build`](../README.md#microtagsbuild)\nremoves each `.render` link from a `define` chain and leaves everything\nelse unchanged.\n\nThere are four ways to run it. All four accept the same two options:\n\n- `keep` -- a list of tag names whose render stays in the bundle. See\n  [Keeping `.render` on the client](#keeping-render-on-the-client).\n- `cascade` -- also remove imports and top-level declarations that are only\n  used by the `.render` function, such as `microtags/htm` or a child component\n  imported for nesting. Without it, those imports stay in the bundle.\n\n### 1. Vite plugin\n\n```ts\n// vite.config.ts\nimport { microtags } from 'microtags/vite'\n\nexport default {\n    plugins: [\n        microtags({ cascade: true }),\n    ],\n}\n```\n\nThe real config for this example, [`vite.config.ts`](./vite.config.ts),\nadds two things to the minimal version above:\n\n- A `filter` regex so the plugin only transforms files in\n  `client/` and `components/`. Server modules keep their render links.\n- `resolve.alias` entries that map `microtags` and `microtags/htm` to\n  `src/`, so the dev server runs against the source instead of `dist/`.\n  Your own project imports the published package and does not need this.\n\n### 2. esbuild plugin\n\n```ts\nimport { build } from 'esbuild'\nimport { microtags } from 'microtags/esbuild'\n\nawait build({\n    entryPoints: ['src/index.ts'],\n    bundle: true,\n    outdir: 'public',\n    plugins: [microtags({ cascade: true })],\n})\n```\n\nBoth plugins take an optional `filter` regex that limits which files are\ntransformed. The default matches `.ts`, `.js`, and `.mjs`.\nTransform warnings are forwarded to the bundler.\n\n\n### 3. `microbuild` CLI\n\nFor a pipeline without a bundler, or to inspect the stripped output:\n\n```sh\n# one file, stdin to stdout\ncat src/counter.ts | microbuild --cascade > dist/counter.ts\n\n# many files, mirrored into --outdir\nmicrobuild 'src/**/*.ts' --outdir dist --cascade --keep my-notice\n```\n\nFiles are never rewritten in place. See\n[`microbuild`](../README.md#microbuild).\n\n\n### 4. `transform` function\n\nThe plugins and the CLI call this. Can use it in a custom build tool or a test.\n\n```ts\nimport { transform } from 'microtags/build'\n\nconst { code, warnings } = transform(source, {\n    cascade: true,\n    filename: 'counter.ts',\n})\n```\n\nThe exact match rule, the unsupported forms, and the warning kinds are\nin the [`microtags/build`](../README.md#microtagsbuild) reference.\n\n\n## Keeping `.render` on the client\n\nSome components must render themselves in the browser, for example a\nnotice that is only shown under certain conditions. There are two ways to\nkeep the render function:\n\n* In source code, pass `{ client: true }` as the second argument to\n  `.render`. The decision travels with the component and applies in every build.\n* At build time, list the tag name in `keep` (the plugin option or the\n  `--keep` flag). The source is unchanged.\n\nA render is kept when either applies. See\n[`client:true` vs `--keep`](../README.md#clienttrue-vs---keep) for when\nto use each.\n\n```ts\nexport const Notice = define('hydra-notice')\n    .withProps(p => ({ message: p.string() }))\n    .render(({ props }) => {\n        return `<p>${props.message}</p>`\n    }, { client: true })\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        // ...\n    })\n```\n\nThe static `render` then exists in the browser, so `html` can render\nthe component on the client. `client/index.ts` does exactly this\nwhen the \"Show notice\" button is clicked:\n\n```ts\nimport { html } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { Notice } from '../components/notice.js'\n\ntarget.innerHTML = html`<${Notice} message=${'Saved'} />`\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> After stripping, the static `render` is `undefined` at runtime even\n> though the type still declares it. Calling `render` on a stripped\n> component in the browser throws.\n# microtags\n\nA helper for building custom elements that hydrate server-rendered HTML.\nThe browser downloads behaviour only; markup is authored once and rendered\non the server.\n\n## Language\n\n### Defining a component\n\n**Component**:\nA custom element defined through the builder chain. It carries behaviour\nand, when it has a render link, its own markup.\n_Avoid_: widget, element (ambiguous with the host instance)\n\n**Tag name**:\nThe custom element name a component is registered under, such as\n`my-counter`. Exposed on the class as `TAG`.\n_Avoid_: element name, selector\n\n**Builder chain**:\nThe sequence of calls that defines a component, starting at `define` and\nending at `setup`.\n_Avoid_: pipeline, config\n\n**Link**:\nOne call in the builder chain: `withProps`, `withRefs`, `withContexts`,\nor `render`. `setup` is the terminal link.\n_Avoid_: step, method (in prose)\n\n**Prop**:\nA named input declared with `withProps`. Inside `setup` it is a signal.\nAn attribute-backed prop is read from the host's HTML attribute of the same\nname; a property-only prop is set from JavaScript only.\n_Avoid_: attribute (that is the HTML side of a prop), input, option\n\n**Coercer**:\nA function that turns a raw attribute string, or its absence, into a typed\nprop value. The four builtins are `number`, `string`, `boolean`, `json`.\n_Avoid_: parser, validator\n\n**Schema prop**:\nA prop validated by a Standard Schema object rather than a builtin coercer.\nA failed validation yields `undefined`.\n_Avoid_: validated prop, custom prop\n\n**Ref**:\nA child element the component declares with `withRefs` and resolves when\nthe host connects. By default a ref is found by its `data-ref` attribute.\n_Avoid_: query, element ref, handle\n\n**Statics**:\nThe values exposed on a component's class for use outside the browser:\n`TAG`, `refs`, `render`, and `props`.\n_Avoid_: metadata, class properties\n\n### Running a component\n\n**Host**:\nThe custom element instance a component's behaviour is attached to. Its\nchildren are light DOM, never a shadow root.\n_Avoid_: root, wrapper, container\n\n**Setup**:\nThe function run once per host when it connects, after refs resolve and\nrequired provided contexts are available.\n_Avoid_: init, mount, constructor\n\n**Setup context**:\nThe object handed to `setup`, carrying the host, prop signals, resolved\nrefs, provided context values, and lifecycle helpers.\n_Avoid_: bare \"context\", ctx (in prose)\n\n**Provided context**:\nA value published by an ancestor host and read by descendants that opt in\nwith `withContexts` or `consume`. Reactive: consumers re-run when the\nprovider updates.\n_Avoid_: bare \"context\", store, global\n\n**Hydration**:\nAttaching behaviour to markup that already exists in the document when the\ncomponent's class is registered.\n_Avoid_: mounting, rendering, upgrading (the browser's term for the\nmechanism, not the intent)\n\n### Rendering markup\n\n**Render function**:\nThe author-supplied function given to the `render` link. It returns the\nhost's inner HTML as a string; the library wraps it in the host tag.\n_Avoid_: template, view\n\n**Render context**:\nThe object handed to the render function: plain prop values, the ref name\nmap, the tag name, and caller-supplied children and attrs. Distinct from\nthe setup context and from provided context.\n_Avoid_: bare \"context\", props (it is more than props)\n\n**Host tag**:\nThe opening and closing markup of the host, with its serialized\nattributes, emitted around the render function's output.\n_Avoid_: wrapper, outer HTML\n\n**Server render**:\nProducing a component's or page's markup as a string outside the browser.\n\"SSR\" is an accepted abbreviation.\n_Avoid_: pre-render, static render\n\n**Client-rendered component**:\nA component whose render link is kept in the browser bundle so it can\nproduce its own markup after page load.\n_Avoid_: dynamic component, lazy component\n\n**Templater**:\nThe `html` tagged template that composes a page from plain tags and\ncomponents, escaping interpolated strings and expanding nested components.\n_Avoid_: template engine, template (that is the HTML element below)\n\n**Template**:\nThe native `<template>` HTML element that `render` and `renderList` from\nthe DOM helpers clone rows from. Unrelated to the templater.\n_Avoid_: template string, partial\n\n**Trusted string**:\nA string the templater inserts without escaping. The output of the\ntemplater, of a render function, and of `raw` are trusted; every other\ninterpolated string is escaped.\n_Avoid_: safe string, raw HTML (that is the function name)\n\n### Building for the client\n\n**Strip**:\nRemoving a component's render link from client code at build time so the\nbrowser does not download markup.\n_Avoid_: tree-shake (that is the bundler's own dead-code pass), prune\n\n**Keep**:\nAn opt-out from stripping, declared in source with `client: true` or at\nbuild time by tag name.\n_Avoid_: preserve, whitelist\n\n**Cascade**:\nAfter stripping, removing the imports and top-level declarations that only\nthe stripped code used, repeated until nothing more is removed.\n_Avoid_: dead-code elimination, tree-shake\ncomposes pages and expands components recursively. It is built on `htm`,\nwhich stays out of the core entry. Interpolated strings are escaped;\nrender output and `raw` strings are trusted.\n\nA Node-only transform in `microtags/build` strips `.render(...)` from the\nclient code. The rule is syntactic: a `.render` link in a call chain\nrooted at the `define` binding imported from `microtags`, with aliases\ntracked. Removed spans become newlines so line numbers survive. An\noptional cascade then removes imports and top-level declarations that only\nthe stripped code used. A component opts out with `{ client: true }` in\nsource or a `keep` list at build time. The parser is the `typescript`\npackage as an optional peer dependency. The transform ships as esbuild and\nVite plugins and as a `microbuild` CLI.\n\nImplementation is tracked under `.scratch/ssr-render-and-microbuild/`.\n\n## Consequences\n\nMarkup and behaviour have one source of truth, and a browser bundle\ncontains hydration code only. Other server templaters, such as Hono, can\ncall the static `render` directly.\n\nThe type declares `render` as always present while it is `undefined` at\nruntime after stripping; this gap is accepted and documented. The build\nstep is opt-in, so import-map consumers ship render code, consistent with\nADR-004. `client: true` is only recognised in an object literal, and the\nnon-chained form `const b = define('x'); b.render(fn)` is unsupported.\nThe transform adds `typescript` as a peer for anyone who uses it. The\ncore size budget stays at 3.5 kB, and the delta from the serializer is\nmeasured and reported.\nhas no Node imports and takes an injectable scaffold loader. In\ndevelopment `@hono/vite-dev-server` runs the app inside Vite, with the\nscaffold passed through Vite's HTML transform. In production a Node\nlistener loads the built scaffold once, mounts the app, and then serves\nbuilt assets with static middleware, so the scaffold is never served\nunassembled.\n\nThe microtags Vite plugin skips modules whose environment consumer is\n`server`, so server builds keep render links.\n\n## Trade-offs\n\nAn HTML source keeps normal document editing and Vite asset handling,\nat the cost of a small outlet contract. A JSX layout would allow\nper-route titles and more outlets, but would make TSX the document\nsource and add a server-side JSX runtime. One outlet and a static title\nare enough for the example.\n\nHono adds a server runtime and development integration. It adds no\nbrowser runtime because it stays out of the client import graph.\n\n## Consequences\n\nSource HTML is distinct from the built template. Client and server are\nbuilt separately into `example-hydration/build/`. Tests cover\nserver/client stripping, complete responses through `app.request()`,\nand the browser against the production listener (Playwright).\n\nThis decision preserves ADR-004's browser-size priority and ADR-005's\ncomponent render methods. It supersedes neither.\nexample-hydration:\nclient/\ncomponents/\nindex.html\npages/\nREADME.md\nserver/\nstate.ts\nvite.config.ts\n\nexample-hydration/client/:\nindex.css\nindex.ts\nutil.ts\n\nexample-hydration/components/:\nbadge.css\nbadge.ts\ncounter.css\ncounter.ts\nnotice.ts\n\nexample-hydration/pages/:\nindex.ts\nlayout.ts\nlist.ts\nnot-found.ts\nnotice.ts\nroutes.ts\n\nexample-hydration/server/:\napp.ts\nlisten.ts\nscaffold.ts\nssr.ts\ntsconfig.json\n89:    \"start:hydration\": \"npm run build:node && vite --config example-hydration/vite.config.ts --open /example-hydration/\",\n91:    \"typecheck:hydration\": \"tsc -p example-hydration/server/tsconfig.json --listFiles false\",\n92:    \"ssr:hydration\": \"esbuild ./example-hydration/server/ssr.ts --bundle --platform=node --format=cjs --loader:.css=empty --define:import.meta.env='{\\\"DEV\\\":false,\\\"PROD\\\":true,\\\"SSR\\\":true,\\\"MODE\\\":\\\"production\\\"}' | node -\",\n94:    \"toc:hydration\": \"markdown-toc --maxdepth 3 -i example-hydration/README.md\",\n99:    \"test:hydration\": \"npm run build:hydration && npx esbuild ./test/hydration.ts --bundle --format=esm --platform=node --packages=external --loader:.css=empty | node --input-type=module && npx esbuild ./test/hydration-listen.ts --bundle --format=esm --platform=node --packages=external | node --input-type=module\",\n100:    \"test:hydration-dev\": \"npm run build:node && npx esbuild ./test/hydration-dev.ts --bundle --format=cjs --platform=node --packages=external | node\",\n101:    \"build:hydration\": \"npm run build:node && vite build --config example-hydration/vite.config.ts && vite build --ssr --config example-hydration/vite.config.ts\",\n102:    \"serve:hydration\": \"node example-hydration/build/server/listen.js\",\n339:A schema prop is attribute-backed and observed, exactly like `p.string()` or\n343:The validator's input is the raw attribute string, or `null` when the attribute\n344:is absent. Because the input is always a string, schemas for non-string values\n441:string and then \"hydrating\" the page on the client.\n456:export function render ():string {\n544:function define (tagName:string):ComponentBuilder\n554:| `.TAG` | `string` | The tag name passed to `define`. See [`.TAG`](#tag). |\n572:* Four built-in coercers turn raw attribute strings into typed values:\n576:| `p.string()` | the raw string | `\"\"` |\n588:    label:   p.string(),           // attribute -> string  ('' when absent)\n613:    user: p.json<{ id:number; name:string }>(),\n621:Like the string/number/boolean validators it is attribute-backed and\n661:    withRefs<R extends Record<string, RefDef>> (\n707:    withContexts<C extends Record<string, ContextToken<unknown>>> (\n740:const ThemeToken = createContext<string>()\n776:string. The render function receives a context with `props` (plain\n806:prop names. `options` accepts `children` (a string of inner HTML) and\n807:`attrs` (an object of extra attributes, all strings).\n1040:type Item = { id:string; name:string; count:number }\n1068:Because the template lives in your HTML instead of a JavaScript string, each\n1071:Nothing concatenates strings into markup, so there is no string-injection\n1091:const ThemeToken = createContext<string>()\n1160:every pass, which creates a vector for string-concatenation XSS.\n1181:        key:(item:T, index:number) => string|number;\n1265:Helpers for reading raw attribute strings and running cleanup\n1283:Each coercion helper takes the raw `getAttribute` result -- a `string` or\n1287:function coerceNumber (raw:string | null):number\n1288:function coerceString (raw:string | null):string\n1289:function coerceBoolean (raw:string | null):boolean\n1290:function coerceJson<T = unknown> (raw:string | null):T | undefined","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d134e0b9-0655-44e9-9843-19fd47b4c489","uuid":"6754a881-bd4d-42e1-b825-f139fcad5729","timestamp":"2026-10-05T00:09:13.068Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Hydration\n\nHydration means writing HTML as a string on the server, then\ncompiling JS for the page to add interactivity. It gets you faster initial page\nload, and the behavior of a\n[single page app](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/SPA).\n\n<details><summary><h2>Contents</h2></summary>\n\n<!-- toc -->\n\n- [Running this example](#running-this-example)\n- [Files in this folder](#files-in-this-folder)\n- [`.render`](#render)\n- [`.html`](#html)\n  * [Self-closing tags](#self-closing-tags)\n  * [`children`](#children)\n  * [Returning `html` from `.render`](#returning-html-from-render)\n- [Nesting components and lists](#nesting-components-and-lists)\n- [Stripping `.render` from the client bundle](#stripping-render-from-the-client-bundle)\n  * [1. Vite plugin](#1-vite-plugin)\n  * [2. esbuild plugin](#2-esbuild-plugin)\n  * [3. `microbuild` CLI](#3-microbuild-cli)\n  * [4. `transform` function](#4-transform-function)\n- [Keeping `.render` on the client](#keeping-render-on-the-client)\n\n<!-- tocstop -->\n\n</details>\n\n\n## Running this example\n\nAll scripts run from the repo root. Each one that uses the Vite config\nbuilds the Node entries (`npm run build:node`) first, because the config\nimports the stripping plugin from `dist/vite.js`.\n\n- `npm run start:hydration` -- start the Vite dev server with the Hono\n  app mounted under `/example-hydration/`, and open the index.\n- `npm run ssr:hydration` -- print one assembled page to stdout. Pass a\n  route after `--`, for example `npm run ssr:hydration -- /list`. The\n  default is the index.\n- `npm run build:hydration` -- production build: a client build and a\n  server build into `build/client` and `build/server` (gitignored).\n- `npm run serve:hydration` -- run the built production server. Set\n  `PORT` to choose the port.\n- `npm run test:hydration` -- build, then test the app with\n  `app.request()` and the built listener.\n- `npm run test:hydration-dev` -- start the dev server and fetch the\n  routes over HTTP.\n- `npm run typecheck:hydration` -- type-check `server/` with Node types.\n- `npm run test:e2e` -- Playwright tests against the production server.\n\nThe Playwright browser is a one-time install:\n\n```sh\nnpx playwright install chromium\n```\n\nThe routes are `/example-hydration/` (a counter with a nested badge),\n`/example-hydration/list` (badges rendered from an array), and\n`/example-hydration/notice` (a button that inserts a client-rendered\nnotice). Any other path under the prefix returns a 404 page.\n\n## Files in this folder\n\nThe folder is split by where each module runs.\n\n- `index.html` -- the checked-in page scaffold, and the Vite HTML entry.\n  `<main>` holds exactly one `<!--microtags:main-->` marker, which the\n  server replaces with the route body. Nothing writes to this file.\n- `vite.config.ts` -- this example's own Vite config: aliases, the\n  stripping plugin, the Hono dev server, and both production builds.\n\n`client/` runs only in the browser:\n\n- `index.ts` -- the client entry. It imports the components, which\n  registers them and hydrates the page, then runs a few runtime\n  assertions and prints the results on the page. The assertions check\n  that `Counter.render` and `Badge.render` are `undefined` in the\n  browser bundle and that `Notice.render` is still a function.\n- `reveal.ts` -- removes the class that hides the page until it has\n  hydrated.\n- `index.css` -- page-level styles.\n\n`components/` runs on both sides:\n\n- `counter.ts` -- a counter with increment and decrement buttons. Its\n  `.render` returns `html`, nests a `Badge` that shows the current\n  count, and passes `children` through.\n- `badge.ts` -- the smallest possible component.\n- `notice.ts` -- keeps its render function on the client with\n  `{ client: true }`.\n- `counter.css`, `badge.css` -- component styles. Under plain esbuild\n  they load as empty modules (`--loader:.css=empty`).\n\n`pages/` runs on both sides:\n\n- `layout.ts` -- the navigation and `<main>` around a page. Each link\n  binds `aria-current` to the `route` signal.\n- `index.ts`, `list.ts`, `notice.ts`, `not-found.ts` -- one page\n  function per route, each taking the app state.\n- `routes.ts` -- the route table. `pageFor(route)` returns the page for\n  a route, or the not-found page.\n\n`server/` runs only in Node:\n\n- `app.ts` -- the Hono app and its routes. It has no Node imports and\n  takes an injectable scaffold loader.\n- `scaffold.ts` -- loads the scaffold and replaces the marker.\n- `listen.ts` -- the production server. It loads the built scaffold,\n  mounts the app, and serves built assets.\n- `ssr.ts` -- the stdout render command.\n- `tsconfig.json` -- type-checks this folder with Node types.\n\n---\n\n## `.render`\n\nWrite your components with a\n[`.render`](../README.md#builderrenderfn-options) method.\nThe function passed to `.render` gets called with a `context` object.\n\nThis package exposes two paths, `microtags/esbuild` and `microtags/vite` that\nprovide plugins that will strip the `.render` function from the client build,\nwhich keeps the clientside bundle small.\n\nAdd a `.render` call in the method chain, before `.setup`. The render function\nreceives `props` (plain values, not signals), `refs`, `TAG`, `children`,\nand `attrs`, and should return the host element's inner HTML.\n\n`props` and `attrs` are both attributes on the tag, split by whether the\nname was declared in `.withProps`. A declared name, like `start` below, is\na prop: it is coerced to its declared type (`5`, a number) and is what\n`.setup` reads on the client. Any other attribute, like `class` or `id`,\nis passed through as a plain string in `attrs`. Both are written to the\nhost tag. `attrs` is there so the render function can react to them, for\nexample to forward `class` to an inner element.\n\n```ts\n// one file for markup and behavior\nimport { signal } from 'alien-signals'\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\n\nexport const HydraCounter = define('hydra-counter')\n    .withProps(p => ({\n        // `start` attribute is coerced to a number\n        start: p.number()\n    }))\n    .withRefs(r => ({\n        display: r.one<HTMLDivElement>(),\n        inc: r.one<HTMLButtonElement>(),\n    }))\n    .render(({ props, refs, attrs }) => {  // serverside only\n        // `class` is not a declared prop, so it arrives in `attrs`\n        const cls = attrs.class ?? ''\n        return `\n            <div data-ref=\"${refs.display}\" class=\"${cls}\">${props.start}</div>\n            <button data-ref=\"${refs.inc}\">+</button>`\n    })\n    .setup(ctx => {  // clientside only\n        const count = signal(ctx.props.start())\n        ctx.effect(() => {\n            ctx.refs.display.textContent = String(count())\n        })\n        ctx.on(ctx.refs.inc, 'click', () => count(count() + 1))\n    })\n```\n\n## `.html`\n\nOn the server, components should be rendered with the\n[`html`](../README.md#microtagshtm)\n[tagged template literal](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Template_literals#tagged_templates),\nor you can call the static `HydraCounter.render(props)` directly.\n`html` knows how to expand every component,\neg `<${MyComponent} start=\"5\"><//>`, into its content, which is the HTML\nreturned by `.render`. The `<//>` is `htm` shorthand for the closing tag,\nso you do not have to repeat the component name.\n\n```ts\n// runs in Node\nimport { html } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { HydraCounter } from './counter.js'\n\nexport function page ():string {\n    return html`<main>\n        <${HydraCounter} start=${5} class=\"big\"><//>\n    </main>`\n}\n```\n\nThe output is the expanded HTML, with props serialized\nas attributes, so `.setup`, which runs on the client, can read the\n`start` attribute. The undeclared `class` attribute is also written to\nthe host tag, and the render function copied it onto the inner `div`.\n\n```html\n<main>\n    <hydra-counter start=\"5\" class=\"big\">\n        <div data-ref=\"display\" class=\"big\">5</div>\n        <button data-ref=\"inc\">+</button>\n    </hydra-counter>\n</main>\n```\n\nCalling the static method directly takes the same two parameters as\nseparate arguments:\n\n```ts\nHydraCounter.render({ start: 5 }, { attrs: { class: 'big' } })\n```\n\nSee [`builder.render`](../README.md#builderrenderfn-options) for the\nfull reference.\n\n### Self-closing tags\n\nInside `html` a component can be written as `<${HydraCounter} start=${5} />`,\nwhich is what the `pages/` modules and `client/index.ts` do. That is fine\nbecause `html` always emits a real closing tag in the output. It is only\nin hand-written HTML that a custom element must not be self-closed, since\nthe browser would parse everything after it as its children.\n\n### `children`\n\nAnything between the open and close tags is passed to the render function\nas `children`, already rendered to a string. `counter.ts` emits\n`${children}` at the end of its markup, so this:\n\n```ts\nhtml`<${Counter} start=${5}><p>Extra content</p><//>`\n```\n\nputs the paragraph after the buttons. The static form is\n`Counter.render({ start: 5 }, { children: '<p>Extra content</p>' })`.\n\n### Returning `html` from `.render`\n\nThe render function can return `html` instead of a plain string. Every\ninterpolated string is escaped, so a prop value containing `<` comes out\nas `&lt;`, which a plain template literal does not do. Nested components\ngo inline as `<${Badge} ... />`. `components/counter.ts` is written\nthis way.\n\nThe one thing to watch is `children`. It reaches the render function as\nan already-rendered string, so passing it through `html` would escape the\nmarkup. Wrap it in `raw()` to insert it as is.\n\n```ts\nimport { define } from 'microtags'\nimport { html, raw } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { Badge } from './badge.js'\n\nexport const Counter = define('hydra-counter')\n    .withProps(p => ({\n        start: p.number(),\n    }))\n    .withRefs(r => ({\n        display: r.one<HTMLDivElement>(),\n        inc: r.one<HTMLButtonElement>(),\n    }))\n    .render(({ props, refs, children }) => {\n        return html`\n            <div data-ref=${refs.display} class=\"count\">${props.start}</div>\n            <${Badge} label=${String(props.start)} />\n            <button data-ref=${refs.inc}>+</button>\n            ${raw(children)}`\n    })\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        // ...\n    })\n```\n\n`html` writes attribute values in quotes for you, so `data-ref=${refs.inc}`\nis enough. Rendering `<${Counter} start=${5}><p>Extra</p><//>` then gives:\n\n```html\n<hydra-counter start=\"5\">\n    <div data-ref=\"display\" class=\"count\">5</div>\n    <hydra-badge label=\"5\"><span>5</span></hydra-badge>\n    <button data-ref=\"inc\">+</button>\n    <p>Extra</p>\n</hydra-counter>\n```\n\nWithout `raw()` the last line would be `&lt;p&gt;Extra&lt;/p&gt;`.\n\n## Nesting components and lists\n\nA render function can nest other components. `counter.ts` puts a\n`Badge` inline in its `html` template. The badge also carries the\ncounter's `current` ref, so `.setup` can update the badge text when the\ncount changes:\n\n```ts\nimport { html } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { Badge } from './badge.js'\n\n// ...\n    .render(({ props, refs, children }) => {\n        return html`\n            <div role=\"status\" class=\"count\">...</div>\n            <div class=\"current\">\n                <span>Current:</span>\n                <${Badge}\n                    data-ref=\"${refs.current}\"\n                    class=\"number\"\n                    label=${String(props.start)}\n                />\n            </div>\n            ...`\n    })\n```\n\n`data-ref` and `class` are not declared props of `Badge`, so they are\nwritten to the `<hydra-badge>` host tag as plain attributes.\n\nBecause `Badge` and `microtags/htm` are only used inside `.render`, they\nare dead code on the client once the render function is stripped. The\n`cascade` option removes those imports too. Without it they stay in the\nbundle. See [Stripping](#stripping-render-from-the-client-bundle).\n\nFor a list, map over the array. Inside an `html` template the array is\nflattened for you:\n\n```ts\nhtml`<ul>\n    ${items.map(item => html`<li><${Badge} label=${item}/></li>`)}\n</ul>`\n```\n\nInside a plain template string, join it yourself:\n\n```ts\n`<ul>\n    ${items.map(item => html`<li><${Badge} label=${item} /></li>`).join('')}\n</ul>`\n```\n\n\n## Stripping `.render` from the client bundle\n\nWithout a build step the render function ships to every browser. The\ntransform function in [`microtags/build`](../README.md#microtagsbuild)\nremoves each `.render` link from a `define` chain and leaves everything\nelse unchanged.\n\nThere are four ways to run it. All four accept the same two options:\n\n- `keep` -- a list of tag names whose render stays in the bundle. See\n  [Keeping `.render` on the client](#keeping-render-on-the-client).\n- `cascade` -- also remove imports and top-level declarations that are only\n  used by the `.render` function, such as `microtags/htm` or a child component\n  imported for nesting. Without it, those imports stay in the bundle.\n\n### 1. Vite plugin\n\n```ts\n// vite.config.ts\nimport { microtags } from 'microtags/vite'\n\nexport default {\n    plugins: [\n        microtags({ cascade: true }),\n    ],\n}\n```\n\nThe real config for this example, [`vite.config.ts`](./vite.config.ts),\nadds two things to the minimal version above:\n\n- A `filter` regex so the plugin only transforms files in\n  `client/` and `components/`. Server modules keep their render links.\n- `resolve.alias` entries that map `microtags` and `microtags/htm` to\n  `src/`, so the dev server runs against the source instead of `dist/`.\n  Your own project imports the published package and does not need this.\n\n### 2. esbuild plugin\n\n```ts\nimport { build } from 'esbuild'\nimport { microtags } from 'microtags/esbuild'\n\nawait build({\n    entryPoints: ['src/index.ts'],\n    bundle: true,\n    outdir: 'public',\n    plugins: [microtags({ cascade: true })],\n})\n```\n\nBoth plugins take an optional `filter` regex that limits which files are\ntransformed. The default matches `.ts`, `.js`, and `.mjs`.\nTransform warnings are forwarded to the bundler.\n\n\n### 3. `microbuild` CLI\n\nFor a pipeline without a bundler, or to inspect the stripped output:\n\n```sh\n# one file, stdin to stdout\ncat src/counter.ts | microbuild --cascade > dist/counter.ts\n\n# many files, mirrored into --outdir\nmicrobuild 'src/**/*.ts' --outdir dist --cascade --keep my-notice\n```\n\nFiles are never rewritten in place. See\n[`microbuild`](../README.md#microbuild).\n\n\n### 4. `transform` function\n\nThe plugins and the CLI call this. Can use it in a custom build tool or a test.\n\n```ts\nimport { transform } from 'microtags/build'\n\nconst { code, warnings } = transform(source, {\n    cascade: true,\n    filename: 'counter.ts',\n})\n```\n\nThe exact match rule, the unsupported forms, and the warning kinds are\nin the [`microtags/build`](../README.md#microtagsbuild) reference.\n\n\n## Keeping `.render` on the client\n\nSome components must render themselves in the browser, for example a\nnotice that is only shown under certain conditions. There are two ways to\nkeep the render function:\n\n* In source code, pass `{ client: true }` as the second argument to\n  `.render`. The decision travels with the component and applies in every build.\n* At build time, list the tag name in `keep` (the plugin option or the\n  `--keep` flag). The source is unchanged.\n\nA render is kept when either applies. See\n[`client:true` vs `--keep`](../README.md#clienttrue-vs---keep) for when\nto use each.\n\n```ts\nexport const Notice = define('hydra-notice')\n    .withProps(p => ({ message: p.string() }))\n    .render(({ props }) => {\n        return `<p>${props.message}</p>`\n    }, { client: true })\n    .setup(ctx => {\n        // ...\n    })\n```\n\nThe static `render` then exists in the browser, so `html` can render\nthe component on the client. `client/index.ts` does exactly this\nwhen the \"Show notice\" button is clicked:\n\n```ts\nimport { html } from 'microtags/htm'\nimport { Notice } from '../components/notice.js'\n\ntarget.innerHTML = html`<${Notice} message=${'Saved'} />`\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> After stripping, the static `render` is `undefined` at runtime even\n> though the type still declares it. Calling `render` on a stripped\n> component in the browser throws.\n# microtags\n\nA helper for building custom elements that hydrate server-rendered HTML.\nThe browser downloads behaviour only; markup is authored once and rendered\non the server.\n\n## Language\n\n### Defining a component\n\n**Component**:\nA custom element defined through the builder chain. It carries behaviour\nand, when it has a render link, its own markup.\n_Avoid_: widget, element (ambiguous with the host instance)\n\n**Tag name**:\nThe custom element name a component is registered under, such as\n`my-counter`. Exposed on the class as `TAG`.\n_Avoid_: element name, selector\n\n**Builder chain**:\nThe sequence of calls that defines a component, starting at `define` and\nending at `setup`.\n_Avoid_: pipeline, config\n\n**Link**:\nOne call in the builder chain: `withProps`, `withRefs`, `withContexts`,\nor `render`. `setup` is the terminal link.\n_Avoid_: step, method (in prose)\n\n**Prop**:\nA named input declared with `withProps`. Inside `setup` it is a signal.\nAn attribute-backed prop is read from the host's HTML attribute of the same\nname; a property-only prop is set from JavaScript only.\n_Avoid_: attribute (that is the HTML side of a prop), input, option\n\n**Coercer**:\nA function that turns a raw attribute string, or its absence, into a typed\nprop value. The four builtins are `number`, `string`, `boolean`, `json`.\n_Avoid_: parser, validator\n\n**Schema prop**:\nA prop validated by a Standard Schema object rather than a builtin coercer.\nA failed validation yields `undefined`.\n_Avoid_: validated prop, custom prop\n\n**Ref**:\nA child element the component declares with `withRefs` and resolves when\nthe host connects. By default a ref is found by its `data-ref` attribute.\n_Avoid_: query, element ref, handle\n\n**Statics**:\nThe values exposed on a component's class for use outside the browser:\n`TAG`, `refs`, `render`, and `props`.\n_Avoid_: metadata, class properties\n\n### Running a component\n\n**Host**:\nThe custom element instance a component's behaviour is attached to. Its\nchildren are light DOM, never a shadow root.\n_Avoid_: root, wrapper, container\n\n**Setup**:\nThe function run once per host when it connects, after refs resolve and\nrequired provided contexts are available.\n_Avoid_: init, mount, constructor\n\n**Setup context**:\nThe object handed to `setup`, carrying the host, prop signals, resolved\nrefs, provided context values, and lifecycle helpers.\n_Avoid_: bare \"context\", ctx (in prose)\n\n**Provided context**:\nA value published by an ancestor host and read by descendants that opt in\nwith `withContexts` or `consume`. Reactive: consumers re-run when the\nprovider updates.\n_Avoid_: bare \"context\", store, global\n\n**Hydration**:\nAttaching behaviour to markup that already exists in the document when the\ncomponent's class is registered.\n_Avoid_: mounting, rendering, upgrading (the browser's term for the\nmechanism, not the intent)\n\n### Rendering markup\n\n**Render function**:\nThe author-supplied function given to the `render` link. It returns the\nhost's inner HTML as a string; the library wraps it in the host tag.\n_Avoid_: template, view\n\n**Render context**:\nThe object handed to the render function: plain prop values, the ref name\nmap, the tag name, and caller-supplied children and attrs. Distinct from\nthe setup context and from provided context.\n_Avoid_: bare \"context\", props (it is more than props)\n\n**Host tag**:\nThe opening and closing markup of the host, with its serialized\nattributes, emitted around the render function's output.\n_Avoid_: wrapper, outer HTML\n\n**Server render**:\nProducing a component's or page's markup as a string outside the browser.\n\"SSR\" is an accepted abbreviation.\n_Avoid_: pre-render, static render\n\n**Client-rendered component**:\nA component whose render link is kept in the browser bundle so it can\nproduce its own markup after page load.\n_Avoid_: dynamic component, lazy component\n\n**Templater**:\nThe `html` tagged template that composes a page from plain tags and\ncomponents, escaping interpolated strings and expanding nested components.\n_Avoid_: template engine, template (that is the HTML element below)\n\n**Template**:\nThe native `<template>` HTML element that `render` and `renderList` from\nthe DOM helpers clone rows from. Unrelated to the templater.\n_Avoid_: template string, partial\n\n**Trusted string**:\nA string the templater inserts without escaping. The output of the\ntemplater, of a render function, and of `raw` are trusted; every other\ninterpolated string is escaped.\n_Avoid_: safe string, raw HTML (that is the function name)\n\n### Building for the client\n\n**Strip**:\nRemoving a component's render link from client code at build time so the\nbrowser does not download markup.\n_Avoid_: tree-shake (that is the bundler's own dead-code pass), prune\n\n**Keep**:\nAn opt-out from stripping, declared in source with `client: true` or at\nbuild time by tag name.\n_Avoid_: preserve, whitelist\n\n**Cascade**:\nAfter stripping, removing the imports and top-level declarations that only\nthe stripped code used, repeated until nothing more is removed.\n_Avoid_: dead-code elimination, tree-shake\ncomposes pages and expands components recursively. It is built on `htm`,\nwhich stays out of the core entry. Interpolated strings are escaped;\nrender output and `raw` strings are trusted.\n\nA Node-only transform in `microtags/build` strips `.render(...)` from the\nclient code. The rule is syntactic: a `.render` link in a call chain\nrooted at the `define` binding imported from `microtags`, with aliases\ntracked. Removed spans become newlines so line numbers survive. An\noptional cascade then removes imports and top-level declarations that only\nthe stripped code used. A component opts out with `{ client: true }` in\nsource or a `keep` list at build time. The parser is the `typescript`\npackage as an optional peer dependency. The transform ships as esbuild and\nVite plugins and as a `microbuild` CLI.\n\nImplementation is tracked under `.scratch/ssr-render-and-microbuild/`.\n\n## Consequences\n\nMarkup and behaviour have one source of truth, and a browser bundle\ncontains hydration code only. Other server templaters, such as Hono, can\ncall the static `render` directly.\n\nThe type declares `render` as always present while it is `undefined` at\nruntime after stripping; this gap is accepted and documented. The build\nstep is opt-in, so import-map consumers ship render code, consistent with\nADR-004. `client: true` is only recognised in an object literal, and the\nnon-chained form `const b = define('x'); b.render(fn)` is unsupported.\nThe transform adds `typescript` as a peer for anyone who uses it. The\ncore size budget stays at 3.5 kB, and the delta from the serializer is\nmeasured and reported.\nhas no Node imports and takes an injectable scaffold loader. In\ndevelopment `@hono/vite-dev-server` runs the app inside Vite, with the\nscaffold passed through Vite's HTML transform. In production a Node\nlistener loads the built scaffold once, mounts the app, and then serves\nbuilt assets with static middleware, so the scaffold is never served\nunassembled.\n\nThe microtags Vite plugin skips modules whose environment consumer is\n`server`, so server builds keep render links.\n\n## Trade-offs\n\nAn HTML source keeps normal document editing and Vite asset handling,\nat the cost of a small outlet contract. A JSX layout would allow\nper-route titles and more outlets, but would make TSX the document\nsource and add a server-side JSX runtime. One outlet and a static title\nare enough for the example.\n\nHono adds a server runtime and development integration. It adds no\nbrowser runtime because it stays out of the client import graph.\n\n## Consequences\n\nSource HTML is distinct from the built template. Client and server are\nbuilt separately into `example-hydration/build/`. Tests cover\nserver/client stripping, complete responses through `app.request()`,\nand the browser against the production listener (Playwright).\n\nThis decision preserves ADR-004's browser-size priority and ADR-005's\ncomponent render methods. It supersedes neither.\nexample-hydration:\nclient/\ncomponents/\nindex.html\npages/\nREADME.md\nserver/\nstate.ts\nvite.config.ts\n\nexample-hydration/client/:\nindex.css\nindex.ts\nutil.ts\n\nexample-hydration/components/:\nbadge.css\nbadge.ts\ncounter.css\ncounter.ts\nnotice.ts\n\nexample-hydration/pages/:\nindex.ts\nlayout.ts\nlist.ts\nnot-found.ts\nnotice.ts\nroutes.ts\n\nexample-hydration/server/:\napp.ts\nlisten.ts\nscaffold.ts\nssr.ts\ntsconfig.json\n89:    \"start:hydration\": \"npm run build:node && vite --config example-hydration/vite.config.ts --open /example-hydration/\",\n91:    \"typecheck:hydration\": \"tsc -p example-hydration/server/tsconfig.json --listFiles false\",\n92:    \"ssr:hydration\": \"esbuild ./example-hydration/server/ssr.ts --bundle --platform=node --format=cjs --loader:.css=empty --define:import.meta.env='{\\\"DEV\\\":false,\\\"PROD\\\":true,\\\"SSR\\\":true,\\\"MODE\\\":\\\"production\\\"}' | node -\",\n94:    \"toc:hydration\": \"markdown-toc --maxdepth 3 -i example-hydration/README.md\",\n99:    \"test:hydration\": \"npm run build:hydration && npx esbuild ./test/hydration.ts --bundle --format=esm --platform=node --packages=external --loader:.css=empty | node --input-type=module && npx esbuild ./test/hydration-listen.ts --bundle --format=esm --platform=node --packages=external | node --input-type=module\",\n100:    \"test:hydration-dev\": \"npm run build:node && npx esbuild ./test/hydration-dev.ts --bundle --format=cjs --platform=node --packages=external | node\",\n101:    \"build:hydration\": \"npm run build:node && vite build --config example-hydration/vite.config.ts && vite build --ssr --config example-hydration/vite.config.ts\",\n102:    \"serve:hydration\": \"node example-hydration/build/server/listen.js\",\n339:A schema prop is attribute-backed and observed, exactly like `p.string()` or\n343:The validator's input is the raw attribute string, or `null` when the attribute\n344:is absent. Because the input is always a string, schemas for non-string values\n441:string and then \"hydrating\" the page on the client.\n456:export function render ():string {\n544:function define (tagName:string):ComponentBuilder\n554:| `.TAG` | `string` | The tag name passed to `define`. See [`.TAG`](#tag). |\n572:* Four built-in coercers turn raw attribute strings into typed values:\n576:| `p.string()` | the raw string | `\"\"` |\n588:    label:   p.string(),           // attribute -> string  ('' when absent)\n613:    user: p.json<{ id:number; name:string }>(),\n621:Like the string/number/boolean validators it is attribute-backed and\n661:    withRefs<R extends Record<string, RefDef>> (\n707:    withContexts<C extends Record<string, ContextToken<unknown>>> (\n740:const ThemeToken = createContext<string>()\n776:string. The render function receives a context with `props` (plain\n806:prop names. `options` accepts `children` (a string of inner HTML) and\n807:`attrs` (an object of extra attributes, all strings).\n1040:type Item = { id:string; name:string; count:number }\n1068:Because the template lives in your HTML instead of a JavaScript string, each\n1071:Nothing concatenates strings into markup, so there is no string-injection\n1091:const ThemeToken = createContext<string>()\n1160:every pass, which creates a vector for string-concatenation XSS.\n1181:        key:(item:T, index:number) => string|number;\n1265:Helpers for reading raw attribute strings and running cleanup\n1283:Each coercion helper takes the raw `getAttribute` result -- a `string` or\n1287:function coerceNumber (raw:string | null):number\n1288:function coerceString (raw:string | null):string\n1289:function coerceBoolean (raw:string | null):boolean\n1290:function coerceJson<T = unknown> (raw:string | null):T | undefined","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
